  "summary": "Recent preprints connect pore-opening edge mechanics, S-shaped vesicle intermediates, protein-induced bilayer deformation and new curvature analysis tools.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the mechanical coupling between lipid bilayers and their open edges or embedded proteins, examined mainly through continuum and coarse-grained simulation. A sharp-interface Stokes-flow model of open membrane pores links line tension, Gaussian rigidity and spontaneous curvature to post-opening remodeling pathways, with hydrodynamic coupling included. Coarse-grained MD combined with Markov state models identifies S-shaped intermediates that give rise to non-standard lipid vesicles, departing from the classical flake-to-vesicle mechanism. For membrane proteins, coarse-grained simulations of ten channel and transporter monomers and oligomers in POPC bilayers indicate that oligomeric interfaces reduce membrane defects, linking bilayer deformation to oligomerization type. MembraneCurvature, a documented MDAnalysis-based Python package, derives membrane surfaces from MD trajectories and computes mean and Gaussian curvature, providing a methodological basis for such analyses. All four items are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
